          "text": "filename: docs/renamephotos.md\n# Rename Holiday Photos with a Bash Loop\n\nRenaming a folder of holiday photos using a bash loop can help organize your images by date, format, or other criteria. This guide provides a step-by-step approach to achieve this using command-line tools.\n\n## Example One: Date Prefix\n\nTo rename photos with a date prefix, you can use a bash loop that appends the date to each filename. This is useful for organizing photos by the date they were taken.\n\n```bash\nfor file in *.jpg; do\n  newname=$(date -r \"$file\" \"+%Y-%m-%d\")_$(basename \"$file\")\n  mv \"$file\" \"$newname\"\ndone\n```\n\n## Example Two: Lowercase Names\n\nIf you want to ensure all filenames are in lowercase, you can modify the loop to convert filenames to lowercase. This helps maintain consistency across different operating systems.\n\n```bash\nfor file in *.jpg; do\n  newname=$(echo \"$file\" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')\n  mv \"$file\" \"$newname\"\ndone\n```\n\n## Example Three: Number the Files\n\nTo number the files sequentially, you can use a counter variable within the loop. This is helpful for creating a consistent naming pattern for all images.\n\n```bash\ncount=1\nfor file in *.jpg; do\n  newname=\"photo_$count.jpg\"\n  mv \"$file\" \"$newname\"\n  count=$((count + 1))\ndone\n```\n\nWrap Up\nRenaming photos with bash loops is a powerful way to organize your files. It allows for automation, consistency, and easy management of large collections. Always ensure you have backups before running renaming scripts.",
